1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,544
Chapter four — experts and skills.

2
00:00:03,604 --> 00:00:11,399
Every node type has a
specialist attached to it.

3
00:00:11,459 --> 00:00:20,287
This one is an Elasticsearch cluster,
so the expert is a search expert.

4
00:00:20,347 --> 00:00:26,288
Ellie knows this node: its dials,
its data shape and its live metrics.

5
00:00:26,348 --> 00:00:36,581
Fourteen experts ship with the workbench — messaging, cache,
search, relational, graph, vector, streaming and the rest.

6
00:00:36,641 --> 00:00:40,585
You can state the objective
in your own words —

7
00:00:40,645 --> 00:00:50,759
or take one of the four the
expert already understands.

8
00:00:50,819 --> 00:01:05,280
Pick one, and it goes to the model
with the whole node as context.

9
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:15,829
It comes back as concrete dial
changes you can accept or ignore.

10
00:01:15,889 --> 00:01:21,928
Two more ways to ask.

11
00:01:21,988 --> 00:01:34,294
Ask is a free-form question answered against
the live model — not a chatbot guessing.

12
00:01:34,354 --> 00:01:54,363
The answer is computed against this
node, not recalled from training data.

13
00:01:55,670 --> 00:02:01,613
And Skill asks the model to size
this node from first principles.

14
00:02:01,673 --> 00:02:05,617
The expert sees the same numbers you do.

15
00:02:05,677 --> 00:02:09,881
That is the difference
between advice and a guess.
